# Toolkits

A toolkit is a command you wrap once and call everywhere — a small CLI, written in a
real systems language, compiled to WebAssembly, and made available as a command that
a [sandbox](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock) or an
[agent](/tooling/authoring-an-agent) can run through `exec`. It's how you extend the
box with tools of your own.

## A toolkit is a wrapped CLI

The body of a `toolkit` block is an ordinary command-line program — it reads stdin
and writes stdout, the classic Unix shape. The platform compiles it to a
wasm32-wasi command module and registers it under its name:

toolkit :upper do
  // summary: uppercase stdin
  #include <stdio.h>
  #include <ctype.h>
  int main(void) { int c; while ((c = getchar()) != EOF) putchar(toupper(c)); return 0; }
end

Once registered, `upper` is a command. An agent told it has an `upper` tool can pipe
text through it; a sandbox with `exec` can invoke it. The toolkit *is* the wasm
command behind that name.

## Many languages, one shape

The language is inferred from the body — C, Rust, and Zig compile to wasm commands
today, each through its real toolchain. Whatever the language, the contract is the
same: stdin in, stdout out. That uniform shape is why a toolkit composes with any
other command and why an agent can use one without knowing how it was written.

A compiled toolkit is content-addressed and cached, so the second build is instant
and the same source always yields the same command.

## Why wrap a CLI at all

Three reasons it's worth the block:

- **Reuse** — write the command once; every agent and sandbox can call it. No
  copy-pasted shell snippets drifting apart.
- **Containment** — the command runs as wasm under the
  [Dock](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock), with only the powers granted. A
  toolkit is a safe way to give an agent real teeth.
- **Legibility** — the tool's source sits in the document next to the agent that uses
  it, parsed by the [one parser](/language/block-grammar) like everything else.

## Toolkits and agents

This is the seam that makes agents useful. An [agent](/tooling/authoring-an-agent)
that "has a bash tool with a web kit" is really an agent granted `exec` over a set of
registered toolkit commands. You build the kit, grant it, and the agent computes real
answers with real tools — inside the wall, every time.
